These focused native tests compile selected functions extracted from the current
Wine server source. They use deterministic allocation, object, window-owner and
shared-memory stubs. They complement, but do not replace, the actual wineserver
and user32 integration tests.

The runner needs Python 3, GCC with AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer,
and an i386 C development environment. From the Wine source root, build into a
temporary directory without changing the source or installed Wine:

    fixture_dir=$(mktemp -d)
    python3 server/native-tests/thread_input/graph.py . --output "$fixture_dir/graph"
    "$fixture_dir/graph-asan"
    "$fixture_dir/graph-32"

The graph fixture inserts the actual create_thread_input, assign_thread_input,
mark_input_component, split_thread_input, repair_thread_input, remove_queue_input,
attach_thread_input and detach_thread_input functions. It also extracts the
cursor, keystate and input teardown sequence from msg_queue_destroy.

Checks cover duplicate, reverse, cyclic and branching attachments, component
membership, input and desktop references, active/focus ownership, cursor and
keystate totals, every allocation failure in attachment and desktop-detachment
operations, every allocation failure while splitting a destroyed queue's input,
repeated deferred-repair failure, deferred repair before attachment, foreground
component retention, and desktop migration with mixed-direction incident edges
or an initially unattached queue.
